\description{
Finds the subset of environmental variables whose distances among the
samples best match the community distances: for each subset size, the
subset with the highest (rank) correlation between its distances and
\code{dm}. This is BIOENV (Clarke & Ainsworth 1993), as \code{vegan::bioenv()} and
scikit-bio's \code{bioenv()} (QIIME 2's \verb{diversity bioenv}) compute it, with
the same results. Every subset is searched, in parallel C.
}
\details{
\strong{Method}

Each variable is standardized as \code{metric} says. For each subset of the
variables, the distances among the samples on just those variables are
correlated with the community distances \code{dm}, over the \eqn{n(n-1)/2}
pairs of samples, as in \code{\link[=mantel]{mantel()}}. For each subset size, the subset
with the highest correlation is kept. Subsets of a size are compared in
the order of \code{utils::combn()} (the order of the variables), and the first
of equally good ones is kept, as in scikit-bio and vegan.

With \code{given}, each correlation is the partial correlation of \code{dm} (\eqn{x})
and the subset's distances (\eqn{y}) given those distances (\eqn{z}),
\deqn{r_{xy.z} = \frac{r_{xy} - r_{xz} r_{yz}}{\sqrt{1 - r_{xz}^2}
  \sqrt{1 - r_{yz}^2}}}
as in \code{\link[=mantel_partial]{mantel_partial()}}. As vegan says, this is strictly correct only
for Pearson.

A subset's correlation is undefined (and never best) when its distances
are all equal, or, for \code{'mahalanobis'}, when its variables are linearly
dependent. A size with no defined correlation has \code{NA} values.

The best subset is chosen to fit \code{dm}, so its correlation is biased
upwards: a Mantel test of it against \code{dm} doesn't give a valid p-value.

\strong{Speed}

There are \eqn{2^p - 1} subsets of \eqn{p} variables (with \code{max_vars = NULL}), so the search doubles in time with each variable: about a million
subsets for 20 variables. \code{max_vars} limits it. Each subset takes one pass
over the pairs of samples (and a sort, for Spearman and Kendall), with no
\eqn{n \times n} matrix, and subsets run in parallel. Results are identical
for any number of \code{cpus}.

\strong{Differences from vegan::bioenv()}
\itemize{
\item \code{dm} must be distances: there's no community table and \code{index}.
Compute them first, as with \code{\link[=bray]{bray()}} (which equals \code{vegdist()}'s
default).
\item Variables come from \code{data} through \code{formula}, or are its numeric
columns, rather than being a separate \code{env} table. Factors aren't
searched, even with \code{'gower'}.
\item \code{given} may also be one value per sample, or a column name of \code{data}.
\item Samples are matched by name between \code{dm}, \code{data}, and \code{given}.
\item The result is the table that \code{summary()} of vegan's result gives.
}

\strong{Same names in other packages}

\code{vegan::bioenv()} has the same name. When both packages are attached,
the one attached last masks the other; call \code{ecodive::bioenv()} to be
sure.
